<h3 id="should_you_upgrade">Should You Upgrade?</h3><br/>
Most A17 Pro &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; owners can comfortably keep their current device. The new model offers the same advertised battery life, storage capacities, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-pencil/">Apple Pencil</a> support, and USB-C transfer speeds. If your &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; still meets your needs, there is little reason to upgrade for everyday performance alone.<br/>
<br/>
The slightly larger display now with &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; technology may be the strongest reason to consider replacing it, particularly if you frequently use your &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; for watching videos or reading. The thinner and lighter design may also make a worthwhile difference if you spend long periods holding it. <br/>
<br/>
For users who regularly encounter distracting reflections or enjoy reading on their device, nano-texture glass could provide a more specific reason to upgrade. It does raise the cost considerably, since it requires at least 256GB of storage as well as the additional &#36;100 display option.<br/>
<br/>
The landscape camera, water resistance, and fast charging are useful additions, but are highly unlikely to justify replacing a A17 Pro model on their own. An upgrade makes more sense when several of these changes address things you find limiting about your current device.<br/>
<br/>
<h3 id="which_model_should_you_buy">Which Model Should You Buy?</h3><br/>
For most new buyers, the A20 Pro model should be the starting point. The &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; display and new design are its main advantages, and both largely matter more to the everyday experience than the chip.<br/>
<br/>
The A17 Pro model may still be worth considering when it offers substantial savings. It retains many of the same capabilities and remains a sensible choice for reading, browsing, taking notes, and watching videos, even if it uses the design of an older device.<br/>
<br/>
